# Claude Code Plugin @claude-code-plugin
{
    @meta {
        type: doc

        sdoc-version: 0.2
    }

    @about {
        The SDOC plugin for Claude Code: what it adds, how to install it for yourself or roll it out to a team, and how it is built and tested. Read it before installing the plugin, when deciding how to distribute it, or before changing anything under `plugins/sdoc-lexica/`.
    }

    @reading-guide {
        {[table]
            Sections | Note
            @what-it-does, @plugin-permissions, @plugin-limits, @install | Installing it for yourself
            @team-rollout, @updates | Rolling it out to a team
            @plugin-goals, @plugin-limits, @plugin-development | Changing the plugin
        }
    }

    # What It Is For @plugin-goals
    {
        The plugin is there to make Claude better at a project's knowledge, by three measures:

        {[.]
            - **Accuracy:** fewer errors, invented facts and stale references in what Claude answers and writes.
            - **Speed:** less time to finish a task.
            - **Tokens:** fewer tokens to finish it.
        }

        A change to the plugin is judged by these, measured by the evals (@plugin-evals) with the plugin and without it. How Claude gets there, such as how many files it opens, is not a goal.
    }

    # What It Does @what-it-does
    {
        The plugin teaches Claude Code to read, write and navigate SDOC files and Lexica knowledge trees, the `lexica/` directories a project keeps its knowledge in. It loads knowledge a section at a time instead of a file at a time, and it puts each file's signposts in front of Claude at the moment they apply.

        It runs inside Claude Code as a **mod**: a hooks module Claude Code executes in its own runtime. No Node.js, Python or other runtime is needed on the machine.

        # Skills @plugin-skills
        {
            {[table]
                Skill | What it covers
                `/sdoc-lexica:authoring` | The syntax, the mistakes that break a file, signposts, and reading a large file by section. The full authoring guide ships with it, to be read by section. Claude loads it on its own only around `.sdoc` files.
                `/sdoc-lexica:lexica` | Finding knowledge in `lexica/` directories, keeping it current (impl-status, specs, conventions), starting a new knowledge file, and handing questions to the research agent (@plugin-research-agent).
            }

            Claude loads a skill when the task calls for it, and either can be invoked by name.
        }

        # Tools @plugin-tools
        {
            Read-only tools Claude calls directly. In a project with a `lexica/` directory they are always listed; elsewhere they wait behind tool search and cost nothing until wanted. Each path a call names gets the permission check a Read of it would get: inside the project they run with no prompt, a deny rule holds, and a path elsewhere is asked about (@plugin-permissions).

            {[table]
                Tool | Answers
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__list` | Every `.sdoc` file under the `lexica/` directories, or the directories given, each with its type, line count, age (@plugin-ages) and about summary. `detail: "titles"` gives titles instead of summaries
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__outline` | A file's sections: id, title, line range and first words, plus the signposts it has. `files` outlines up to 20 at once, `depth: 1` shows the top-level sections only, and `previews: false` leaves out the first words
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__read` | Sections by id, as source with line numbers. The file's own id reads it whole, or answers a large file's outline; an unknown id answers the ids the file has. To change what it shows, Claude Reads those lines first: Edit refuses a file Read has not opened
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__search` | The sections that best match a query, best first (@plugin-search). `read: true` adds the best sections whole
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__guides` | A file's about, reading guide, related resources and editing guide
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__check` | Parse errors and signpost, reference and citation findings, as `build-doc --check` reports them
                `mcp__sdoc-lexica__backlinks` | What links to a file, or to one of its sections (`file.sdoc#id`, or `id`), each link with its line and the section it sits in. For use before renaming, moving or removing a section or a file
            }

            # Search @plugin-search
            {
                Search ranks sections as a search engine does (BM25): a rare word, a word in the title and a short section count for more, and a file's about summary half as much again as a section. A word also matches inside a longer one (`fsync` finds `fsynced`). A repeated word counts once, and a one-letter word is dropped unless it is all there is.

                {[.]
                    - When no section holds every word of a query of two or more, it lists those that hold the most, each marked with the words it lacks (`[no "fsync"]`). When fewer than three hold the most, those one word short follow, as long as that is still half the words.
                    - It shows the best 20, and says when there were more.
                    - `read: true` adds the best sections whole, in rank order: up to 5, within 6,000 characters. One that does not fit is named with its line range, never cut.
                }
            }

            # Backlinks @plugin-backlinks
            {
                Without an id, `backlinks` lists the links to a file from other files. With one (`file.sdoc#id`, or `id`), it lists the links to that section or a section inside it, by id or title slug, including the file's own `@id` references and `#id` links; a reference from within the section to a section inside it moves with it, so it is left out. Links in signposts count, web links do not. When the section has sections inside it, each link to one of them says which, and a last line names those nothing links to, so none needs asking about on its own. It looks in the `lexica/` directories or the `dirs` given, and for a section in its own file too.
            }
        }

        # Hooks @plugin-hooks
        {
            {[table]
                When | What the plugin adds
                The first prompt of a conversation, in a project with `lexica/` | An index of the knowledge files, each with its summary and age, and the tools that load them by section, within `indexSize` (@plugin-settings). A summary is never cut: when they do not all fit, files are listed by title, and past that some are left out, with a count
                Claude starts a subagent, in a project with `lexica/` | The index, within 3,000 characters, added to the subagent's task. A fork, a resumed agent and a workflow's agent get none
                The first time Claude opens an `.sdoc` file, by Read or by the outline, read or guides tool | The file's reading guide and related resources, when it has them
                A Read of a file over 300 lines, whole | The file's outline, to read by section next time
                A prompt mentions `@lexica/file.sdoc#id` | Only that section, by id or title slug. The document's own id attaches the file whole, and so does an id it lacks, with the ids it has; `#L10-20` is Claude Code's own. The file's reading guide comes with it, as with a Read
                You type `#` after a mention of an `.sdoc` file | The file's section ids, below Claude Code's suggestions: those starting with the text typed first, then those whose id or title holds it, up to 15
                The first edit of a file with an editing guide | The edit is applied, and its result carries the guide and the sections the guide links to, as a Bash change's does. Not when the guide was shown already: a Read whose lines held it, the `guides` tool, or a `read` or `search` answer that held it. Nothing is refused (@plugin-limits)
                Every edit of an `.sdoc` file | The findings the edit added (a parse error, a broken reference, a signpost of the wrong shape), and nothing about what was already there
                A Bash command that names an `.sdoc` file and changes it (`sed -i`, a script) | What an edit gets, after the change: the editing guide and its linked sections the first time, and the findings the change added
            }

            The index comes once per conversation, and a file's guides and outline once per file and agent (a subagent gets its own). A compaction or a clear starts over from the next prompt, since what was shown went with it, except a compaction partway through a long turn, which can go unseen (@plugin-limits). In a project with no `.sdoc` files the plugin adds nothing.

            The sections an editing guide links to come whole, up to 6,000 characters in all; one that does not fit is named with its line range. Links and mentions are followed only to `.sdoc` files inside the project.

            A Bash command is watched for the files it names, up to 20; one it changes without naming it goes unseen (@plugin-limits).
        }

        # The Research Agent @plugin-research-agent
        {
            In a project with a `lexica/` directory, the plugin registers a subagent, `sdoc-lexica:research`, that Claude can hand a question about the project's knowledge; the sections it reads stay out of the main conversation. It answers in at most 200 words, the answer first, citing each claim as `path @id (Lx-y)`, and says what the knowledge does not cover. It is read-only (the plugin's tools, Read, Grep and Glob) and sets no model of its own.

            It is for a question whose answer may be spread over several files, or whose place Claude does not know. Its description steers Claude away from it when Claude already knows where to look and expects the answer in one place: the agent starts with none of the conversation, so a question one section answers costs more and takes longer through it than read directly.
        }

        # How Old a File Is @plugin-ages
        {
            The index and `list` give each file's age, `(212 lines, changed 3 weeks ago)`, from the latest commit that touched it, never from its modification time, which a checkout resets. One `git log` runs per index or list, within 3 seconds, and its answer is kept a minute.

            {[.]
                - An uncommitted edit shows the last commit's age; an untracked file shows none.
                - In a shallow clone, a file not changed within the fetched history shows the oldest fetched commit's age.
                - Outside a git repository, without git, when git takes over 3 seconds, or for a directory outside the project, no age is shown.
            }

            Each file's description (type, title, line count, about) is kept in the plugin's store between sessions, and a file is parsed again only when its size or modification time changes, or when an update of the plugin changed its parser. When the store's 4 MiB fill up, the oldest project's entry is dropped. With `verbose` on, a second session shows `descriptions: 12 of 12 knowledge files from the cache, 0 read`. Within a conversation, the `lexica/` directories are found once (again after `/clear` or a compaction), and `search` and `backlinks` read and parse a file again only when its size or modification time changes. A `lexica/` directory added mid-conversation is seen in the next one, or at once in a project that had none.
        }
    }

    # Settings @plugin-settings
    {
        Set them with `/plugin configure sdoc-lexica@sdoc` in a session, or from a shell by piping JSON to `claude plugin configure sdoc-lexica@sdoc --values-stdin`. A change made in a session applies at once: `verbose` from the plugin's next line, `indexSize` from the next index it sends (the next conversation, or after a `/clear` or a compaction), since the index already in the conversation stays. A change made from a shell applies to sessions started after it.

        {[table]
            Setting | Values | What it does
            `indexSize` | `6k`, `12k` (default), `24k` | The most the knowledge index adds to a conversation's first prompt, in characters: about 1,500, 3,000 or 6,000 tokens. How many files fit with their summaries depends on how long the summaries are: at 12k, about 40 files whose summaries are two or three sentences, and about half that with longer ones.
            `verbose` | `false` (default), `true` | Shows each thing the plugin does as a line in the session (@plugin-verbose).
        }
    }

    # Permissions @plugin-permissions
    {
        A call the plugin answers itself never reaches Claude Code's permission check, so the plugin asks that check itself: each path a call names, as written and where it leads through links, is put to it as a Read, or for a directory as a Glob of the `.sdoc` files under it.

        {[table]
            The check answers | Inside the project or the plugin | Elsewhere
            deny | Refused | Refused
            allow | Answered | Answered
            ask | Answered, as a Read there is | You are asked
        }

        {[.]
            - **Deny rules hold inside the project too.** With `Read(./secrets/**)` in `permissions.deny`, `outline` of a file under `secrets/` is refused. So does a rule naming the tool, such as `mcp__sdoc-lexica__search`.
            - **Elsewhere, you are asked** once per call: Allow once, Allow for this session (what the question named: that file, or for a directory everything under it, until the session ends), or Deny. Anything typed instead refuses the call, and Claude is told what you wrote.
            - **With no one to ask** (`claude -p`, or the question dismissed), a path elsewhere is refused, and Claude is pointed to Read.
            - **Every file the plugin reads unasked is checked too, and left out on a deny.** `list`, `search` and `backlinks` check each `.sdoc` file of the directories they walk, the `lexica/` ones by default included, and say how many they left out; so does the index Claude is given, which then lists none of a denied file's summary. A denied file is not followed from an editing guide, not watched when an Edit or a command changes it, and offers no ids after `#`. With `Read(./lexica/private/**)` denied, nothing under `lexica/private/` reaches Claude through the plugin.
            - **Symbolic links are followed inside the project only.** A walk (the index, `list`, `search`, `backlinks`) follows a link to a file or a directory inside the project, so a linked `lexica/` or a linked `.sdoc` file is found, and a `lexica/` reached both through a link and as itself is listed once. A link that leads outside the project is left out, and the answer says how many; a loop, or a link into a directory the walk already covers, is passed over.
        }
    }

    # Seeing It Work @plugin-verbose
    {
        What the plugin hands Claude, such as the index, a reading guide or an edit's findings, is never shown in the session. Two ways to see it work:

        {[.]
            - **The verbose option.** Each thing the plugin does is shown as one dim line in the session, which Claude is not sent: what it attached (the index, a brief, guides, an outline, findings), what it refused, narrowed or allowed, and where file descriptions came from. A tool's plain answer has no line, since the session shows the call. Turn it on with `/plugin configure sdoc-lexica@sdoc` in a session, where it applies at once, or from a shell, where it applies to the sessions started after it:
            {
                ```
echo '{"verbose":"true"}' | claude plugin configure sdoc-lexica@sdoc --values-stdin
                ```
            }
            - **The debug log.** The same lines are always written there, verbose or not, together with the engine's own: the module loading, each tool registered, each hook's duration, and any hook skipped and why.
            {
                ```
claude --debug-file ~/sdoc-debug.log
tail -f ~/sdoc-debug.log | grep --line-buffered sdoc-lexica
                ```
            }
        }

        `claude plugin details` reports no hooks and no tools for this plugin. It does not count what a mod sets up when a session starts; the plugin is not missing anything.
    }

    # Known Limitations @plugin-limits
    {
        What the plugin does not do, or not reliably, and why each is left as it is.

        {[.]
            - **A compaction partway through a long turn can go unseen.** Claude Code does not tell a user plugin that it compacted, so the plugin infers it at the next prompt, from a context smaller than at the prompt before. When the context has grown back past that size by the time the turn ends, the compaction goes unseen, and the index and the guides are not shown again in that conversation. Several ways of inferring it were tried, and none was reliable. It will be closed when Claude Code reports a compaction to a user plugin.
            - **An editing guide is never enforced.** The edit is applied, and the guide comes in its result. A model that does not go back to its edit leaves what the guide would have changed. The plugin first refused the first edit of a guided file once; that cost a round trip on every rename in the evals, where the guide was about something else, and was dropped.
            - **A file a Bash command changes without naming it goes unseen,** as through a glob or a `find`; and a command is watched for 20 files at most.
            - **A skill run in a fork is briefed although it has the conversation.** Its spawn raises no event the plugin sees, and Claude Code does not list it as a fork, so its first tool call brings the index, about 750 tokens. Telling it apart would mean reading its conversation, which can cost more than the brief.
            - **A helper agent in its own worktree is briefed from the main checkout.** The worktree is made after the brief is written, and nothing says there will be one. The copy starts from the last commit, so the brief can list a file the copy lacks, or an older summary; the helper's tools read its copy.
            - **A `lexica/` directory added mid-conversation,** in a project that already had one, is seen in the next conversation, or after a `/clear` or a compaction.
            - **Every walk checks each file's permission.** The index, a brief, and each `list`, `search` and `backlinks` call put every `.sdoc` file they walk to the permission check, with a look at where it leads, and nothing is remembered between calls. Not measured on a large tree yet: the index's and each tool's line in the debug log (@plugin-verbose) say how many files a walk checked and how long it took.
            - **Ages come from commits.** An edit not committed yet shows the last commit's age, an untracked file none, and in a shallow clone a file older than the history fetched shows the oldest fetched commit's age. Outside a repository, without git, when git takes over 3 seconds, or for a directory outside the project, no file has an age.
            - **The skills are listed in every project,** with a `lexica/` directory or not: a plugin cannot hide a skill per project. The lexica skill's description says the index announces a project's knowledge, so where there is no index there is none to look for.
            - **Mentions, autocomplete and the permission question are checked by hand only.** The tests drive the hooks' code, not Claude Code raising them (@plugin-testing).
        }

        Not verified yet:

        {[.]
            - the earliest Claude Code version that runs the plugin's hooks module; it is built and tested on 2.1.312;
            - that a repository's `.claude/settings.json` loads the plugin with no install step (@team-rollout);
            - Cowork and claude.ai, where only the absence of a top-level `bin/` folder has been checked.
        }
    }

    # Installing It @install
    {
        In a Claude Code session:

        ```
/plugin install sdoc-lexica --marketplace entropicwarrior/sdoc
        ```

        Answer `y` to add the marketplace, then pick a scope: user, for every project. From a shell, the same is `claude plugin install sdoc-lexica --marketplace entropicwarrior/sdoc`.

        The repository holds the whole SDOC project. To fetch only what the plugin needs, add the marketplace with a sparse checkout first:

        ```
claude plugin marketplace add entropicwarrior/sdoc --sparse .claude-plugin plugins
claude plugin install sdoc-lexica@sdoc
        ```

        # Requirements @plugin-requirements
        {
            {[.]
                - A recent Claude Code. The plugin is built and tested on 2.1.312.
                - Mods enabled. An organisation's managed settings can switch them off. The skills still load then, but the tools and hooks do not.
            }

            The mod interface is in early access and can change between Claude Code releases. When an update breaks the plugin, `claude plugin validate` on its folder names what changed.
        }
    }

    # Rolling It Out to a Team @team-rollout
    {
        Two settings make Claude Code offer the plugin to everyone: the marketplace, and the plugin enabled from it. Pin the marketplace to `main`, which carries releases only; the default branch, `develop`, moves between them.

        ```json
{
  "extraKnownMarketplaces": {
    "sdoc": {
      "source": { "source": "github", "repo": "entropicwarrior/sdoc", "ref": "main" },
      "autoUpdate": true
    }
  },
  "enabledPlugins": { "sdoc-lexica@sdoc": true }
}
        ```

        {[.]
            - **For a whole organisation:** an administrator puts this in Claude Code's managed settings (in the claude.ai admin console, or the `managed-settings.json` a device manager deploys). Every member gets the plugin, and cannot turn its marketplace's auto-update off.
            - **For one repository:** commit it as `.claude/settings.json`. Each person who opens the repository and trusts the folder should get the plugin for that project; this is not verified yet (@plugin-limits).
        }
    }

    # Updates @updates
    {
        The plugin sets no version number, so Claude Code versions it by commit. Every commit on the branch you track is an update, and on `main` that means every release and every plugin fix merged there. With auto-update on, Claude Code fetches it in the background and asks for `/reload-plugins`. Without it, run `claude plugin update sdoc-lexica@sdoc`, then `/reload-plugins` in a running session.

        `/plugin` therefore shows a commit rather than a version number, and an SDOC release that leaves the plugin unchanged still counts as an update.
    }

    # Changing the Plugin @plugin-development
    {
        # Layout @plugin-layout
        {
            {[table]
                Path | Holds
                `.claude-plugin/marketplace.json` | The marketplace, listing `plugins/sdoc-lexica`
                `plugins/sdoc-lexica/hooks/register.mjs` | The hooks module: binds Claude Code's events to the handlers
                `plugins/sdoc-lexica/hooks/handlers.mjs` | Everything the plugin does, written against a small file-access interface so Node tests can drive it
                `plugins/sdoc-lexica/skills/` | The two skills
                `plugins/sdoc-lexica/lib/` | Generated ES-module copies of `src/sdoc.js` and `src/knowledge-base.js`
                `src/knowledge-base.js` | Discovery, outlines, section reads, ranked search, backlinks, signposts and checks over SDOC text; runtime-neutral, and shared with `tools/build-doc.js`
                `test/plugin-evals/` | The evals (@plugin-evals): the cases under `evals/`, the fixture project and `compare.js`. Under `test/`, so never shipped
            }
        }

        # Rules @plugin-rules
        {
            {[.]
                - **Nothing the hooks module loads may use Node.** No `require`, `fs`, `path` or `process`: Claude Code runs the module without them. Files are reached through `$.fs`, in `register.mjs` only.
                - **Edit the sources, never `plugins/sdoc-lexica/lib/`.** Run `npm run build:plugin` after changing `src/sdoc.js`, `src/knowledge-base.js` or the authoring guide, and commit what it writes; a marketplace install runs no build step. The tests fail while a generated file is stale.
                - **Never give the plugin a version.** Not in `plugin.json`, and not in the marketplace entry. Once a version is set, Claude Code updates the plugin only when that number changes, so a fix shipped without a bump reaches nobody, and nothing says so. Without one, every commit is a version. `claude plugin validate` warns that no version is set; that warning is expected.
                - **Four events never reach the plugin.** Claude Code's built-in security plugin passes over a user plugin's hooks on `prompt.context`, `session.compact`, `session.append` for a compaction's rows, and the classic `SessionStart` (so a hook matching `source: "compact"`, the usual way to see a compaction, is closed to it). The index goes with the first prompt instead, and a compaction or a clear is seen at the next prompt: the conversation's opening message has changed, since the summary took its place. The transcript is fetched to see it only when `$.session.usage()`, which costs nothing, says one may have happened: a new `startedAt` (a clear), or a context token count that is missing, as it is from a compaction until the next response, or lower than at the last prompt. A transcript past the 4,096 messages `$.session.messages` returns is not taken for a compaction.
                - **`agent.spawn` does reach it.** Checked on 2.1.312: the debug log says `agent.spawn general-purpose: prompt rewritten by a hook`, and nothing about it being bypassed. A `tool.call` fallback in `register.mjs` briefs, at its first tool call, any subagent the spawn hook did not see: one a release passes over the hook for, or one whose spawn raises none.
                - **A tool call the plugin answers skips Claude Code's permission check.** That check runs beneath the step the plugin's hook does not take, so `consent()` in `handlers.mjs` puts the question to it instead: every path the call names, as written and where it leads through links, as a Read or a Glob (@plugin-permissions). A new tool names its paths in `toolPaths`, and a parameter that names a directory needs the kind `dir` in `toolRequests`, or it is checked as a file.
                - **Call each `$` noun where it is written.** The engine accepts no other form: `claude plugin validate` refuses `$.store` passed as a value. That is why `ioOf` in `register.mjs` spells out every call.
                - **A skill is named after its folder.** `skills/lexica/` is `/sdoc-lexica:lexica`; a `name:` in the frontmatter does not change it, so the two must agree.
            }
        }

        # Shipping a Fix @plugin-hotfix
        {
            The mod interface is in early access, so a Claude Code update can break the plugin with no SDOC change at all. A fix for that does not wait for a release:

            {[#]
                1. Branch `hotfix/plugin-<what>` from `main`.
                2. Fix it, run `npm test`, and check it by hand as @plugin-testing describes.
                3. Open a pull request to `main`. Once it is merged, coworkers tracking `main` have the fix with their next update.
                4. Merge `main` back into `develop`.
            }

            Publish no GitHub release for it. The npm and VS Code Marketplace publishing runs on a release, and nothing outside the plugin changed.
        }

        # Testing @plugin-testing
        {
            `npm test` runs `test/test-plugin.js`, which covers:

            {[.]
                - the generated files and manifests;
                - that the ES-module parser builds the same tree as `src/sdoc.js` for every file in the repository;
                - the knowledge-base functions;
                - every hook and tool through an in-memory file system.
            }

            The tests cannot show the engine calling the hooks. Before a release that changes the plugin, check it by hand:

            ```
claude plugin validate plugins/sdoc-lexica
claude plugin validate .
claude --plugin-dir plugins/sdoc-lexica
            ```

            In that session, in a project with a `lexica/` directory:

            {[.]
                - ask what knowledge index Claude was given, and look for the ages in it;
                - Read a file with a reading guide;
                - edit a file with an editing guide (applied, the guide in its result);
                - break a reference in an edit and look for the finding;
                - change a file with an editing guide through Bash (`sed -i`), and look for the guide after the change;
                - run `/compact`, and ask again what knowledge index Claude was given;
                - have Claude start a subagent, then look in the debug log for `prompt rewritten by a hook` on the `agent.spawn` line, and for no `bypassed` there;
                - mention `@lexica/<file>.sdoc#<id>` and ask what was attached (that section only); mention `#nope` (the whole file, and the ids it has); type `#` after a mention and look for the ids offered;
                - with `Read(./secrets/**)` in `permissions.deny`, call `outline` on a file under `secrets/` (refused);
                - call a tool on a file in the project (answered, with no prompt) and on one outside it (a question: allow it once, then for the session, and deny it);
                - under `claude -p`, call a tool on a file outside the project (refused);
                - with `verbose` on, start a second session and look for `descriptions: N of N knowledge files from the cache`;
                - with `verbose` on, watch a line appear for each of these.
            }

            `claude --debug-file <path>` logs every hook the engine ran, skipped or refused.
        }

        # Evals @plugin-evals
        {
            The tests show what the plugin does; the evals show whether it helps, by the three goals (@plugin-goals). Each case is a task Claude carries out in a small project with a `lexica/` directory, run with the plugin and without it. Accuracy is the graders' score, each grader checking an outcome (the answer, the files it edited) the same way in both runs; speed is the wall time of a run; tokens are counted from its trace in three kinds, input at full price, input re-read from the prompt cache (about a tenth of the price) and output, with the run's price beside them. `compare.js` reports them per case and for the suite. A case is never chosen, nor a grader written, because the plugin wins it.

            The project's knowledge is 23 files, about 5,800 lines, among them a 2,700-line format specification and documents that are out of date and say so, whose old values a text search finds next to the current ones. The eleven cases cover finding a fact that an outdated document states differently, finding a skill, citing across two files, a question that shares few words with the section that answers it, finding the links to a section before changing it, renaming an id and fixing every link to it, an edit an editing guide governs, creating a new file, telling which file is stale from git history alone, handing research to a subagent, and staying out of the way in a project with no `lexica/`.

            ```
npm run eval:plugin
            ```

            This runs `claude plugin eval` on the repository root with `--eval-dir test/plugin-evals/evals` and the plugin trusted; add `--model haiku`, `sonnet` or `opus` after `--`. Results are not committed. `test/plugin-evals/README.md` has the commands for a run before and after a change, and `compare.js` prints them side by side.

            {[.]
                - **Runs are capped by turns, not money.** Each case sets `max_turns: 12` and `timeout_seconds: 300`. A pass on all three models, with and without the plugin, cost about $16 at the time of writing.
                - **The target is the repository root.** A case may load only plugins under the target, and `--eval-dir` refuses a path outside it, so the cases name `plugins/sdoc-lexica` from the root.
                - **The fixture is never a directory named `lexica/`.** It is stored as `fixture/knowledge/`, and each case's scaffold copies it to `lexica/` in a new git repository, with commits backdated so that the ages are real. Stored as `lexica/`, it would be taken for this repository's own knowledge.
            }
        }
    }
}
